            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have proved that for \\(n=3\\) there is **no** configuration of three distinct lines containing exactly \\(k=2\\) sunny lines that covers all required points. Hence the desired configuration is impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Identify the required points.**  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the condition “\\(a+b\\le n+1\\)” yields the six points  \n   \\[\n   T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Collinearity analysis.**  \n   The only lines that contain three points of \\(T_3\\) are  \n   \\[\n   y=1,\\qquad x=1,\\qquad x+y=4,\n   \\]\n   all of which are *non‑sunny* (horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   *Lemma 1.* A sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(T_3\\).  \n\n   *Lemma 2.* A non‑sunny line can contain at most three points of \\(T_3\\), and if it contains three points it must be one of the three lines above.\n\n3. **Possible non‑sunny line.**  \n   Since exactly two of the three lines must be sunny, the remaining line is non‑sunny.  \n   A non‑sunny line that covers at least two points of \\(T_3\\) can only be one of the following six possibilities (the three three‑point lines and three two‑point lines):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{Line} & \\text{Points of }T_3\\text{ it covers}\\\\ \\hline\n   y=1 & (1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\\\\n   x=1 & (1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\\\\n   x+y=4 & (1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\\\\n   y=2 & (1,2),(2,2)\\\\\n   x=2 & (2,1),(2,2)\\\\\n   x+y=3 & (1,2),(2,1)\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   Any other non‑sunny line meets at most one point of \\(T_3\\); then the two sunny lines could cover at most \\(2+2=4\\) points, leaving at least two uncovered – impossible. Hence we need only examine the six lines above.\n\n4. **Exhaustive case analysis.**  \n   For each candidate non‑sunny line we list the points of \\(T_3\\) that remain uncovered and show that they cannot be covered by two sunny lines (each sunny line can contain at most two points by Lemma 1).  \n   The analysis proceeds as follows.\n\n   *Case \\(y=1\\).* Uncovered points: \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\).  \n   Any pair among them is either vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\) – all non‑sunny. Hence no sunny line can cover two of these points, and we cannot cover all three with only two sunny lines.  \n\n   *Case \\(x=1\\).* Uncovered points: \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n   Again every possible pair is prohibited (vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\n   *Case \\(x+y=4\\).* Uncovered points: \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n   All three possible pairs are prohibited for the same reason.  \n\n   *Case \\(y=2\\).* Uncovered points: \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \n   The four points must be split into two unordered pairs for the two sunny lines. The three possible partitions are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(1,1)-(1,3),\\;(2,1)-(3,1)\\},\\;\n   \\{(1,1)-(2,1),\\;(1,3)-(3,1)\\},\\;\n   \\{(1,1)-(3,1),\\;(1,3)-(2,1)\\}.\n   \\]  \n   Their slopes are respectively vertical, horizontal, vertical + horizontal, and vertical + horizontal, while the last partition contains the pair \\((1,3)-(2,1)\\) of slope \\(-2\\) (sunny) but the other pair \\((1,1)-(3,1)\\) is horizontal (non‑sunny). Thus no partition yields two sunny lines.  \n\n   *Case \\(x=2\\).* Uncovered points: \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),