e \\(k=0,1\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H:\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V:\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D:\\;x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The value \\(k=0\\) is always possible  \n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) has a \\(y\\)-coordinate between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\); hence it lies on exactly one of the \\(L_i\\).  \nAll \\(L_i\\) are parallel to \\(\\mathcal H\\), therefore none of them is sunny.  \nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The value \\(k=1\\) is always possible  \n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) except \\((1,n)\\) are covered.  \nThe line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y-n=2(x-1)\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y=2x+n-2\n\\]\n\npasses through \\((1,n)\\); its slope is \\(2\\), so \\(L\\) is sunny.  \nConsequently we have a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) (valid when \\(n\\ge 4\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n\n*Step\u202fa.*  Use the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k .\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n*Step\u202fb.*  The uncovered points form  \n\n\\[\nT_k'=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\]\n\na triangular set of size \\(|T_k'|=k(k+1)/2\\).\n\n*Step\u202fc.*  For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \