(T_n\\) has \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), so it lies on exactly one of the horizontal lines. All lines are non‑sunny. No issue.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\n> “Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\). … The line  \n> \\[\n> L:\\;y-n=2(x-1)\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y=2x+n-2\n> \\]  \n> passes through \\((1,n)\\); its slope is \\(2\\), so \\(L\\) is sunny.”\n\n*Verification:* Correct. The horizontal lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) except \\((1,n)\\); the added line indeed passes through \\((1,n)\\) and has slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\). Hence exactly one sunny line. No issue.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Claim about uncovered points for \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\n> “Fix \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n> *Step a.* Use the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\).  \n> *Step b.* The uncovered points form  \n> \\[\n> T_k'=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n> \\]”\n\n**Issue – Critical Error.**  \nAfter removing the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\), the remaining points are those with \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Setting \\(b' = b-(n-k)\\) (so \\(b'\\ge1\\)) we obtain the condition \\(a+b'\\le k+1\\). Thus the uncovered set is **a vertical translation** of \\(T_k'\\), namely \\(\\{(a,b'+n-k): a,b'>0,\\ a+b'\\le k+1\\}\\). It is not equal to \\(T_k'\\) as claimed. This false identification invalidates the subsequent construction.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Definition of the sunny lines \\(L_i\\)\n\n> “For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n> \\[\n> L_i:\\; y-1=\\frac{i}{\\,k+1-i\\,}\\,(x-i) .\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]”\n\n*Verification of definition:* The formula defines a line with slope \\(\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\) passing through \\((i,1)\\). The slope is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) and \\(k\\ge2\\); thus each \\(L_i\\) is indeed sunny. This part is correct.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Claim that the lines \\(L_i\\) cover all points of \\(T_k'\\)\n\n> “We claim that the 