 of coverage.** Because the above reasoning is invalid, the statement that the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) cover all points of \\(T_k'\\) is unsupported.\n\nThus the entire construction for \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) collapses.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Statement “distinct points lie on distinct lines …”\n\n> “Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) together cover the whole set \\(T_k'\\).”\n\n**Issue – Critical Error.** This conclusion depends on the flawed coverage argument in the previous step. Since the coverage claim is false, the conclusion is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Impossibility proof for \\(k=n\\) – initial observation\n\n> “Consider the set  \n> \\[\n> D_n=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\subset T_n .\n> \\]  \n> Any two distinct points of \\(D_n\\) lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\), whose slope is \\(-1\\); therefore **no sunny line can contain two points of \\(D_n\\)**.”\n\n*Verification:* Correct. A sunny line cannot be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); the line through any two points of \\(D_n\\) has slope \\(-1\\), so it is not sunny. Hence each sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(D_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 9. Choice of a line through \\((1,1)\\)\n\n> “Pick the point \\((1,1)\\in T_n\\). It lies on some line \\(L\\in\\mathcal L\\), say the line joining \\((1,1)\\) and \\((i,n+1-i)\\) for a certain \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). The slope of this line is  \n> \\[\n> m=\\frac{n+1-i-1}{i-1}= \\frac{n-i}{i-1}.\n> \\tag{5}\n> \\]”\n\n**Issue – Critical Error.** The argument proceeds to claim a contradiction with the earlier observation about \\(D_n\\). However:\n- The line \\(L\\) contains exactly **one** point of \\(D_n\\) (namely \\((i,n+1-i)\\)); the other point \\((1,1)\\) is **not** in \\(D_n\\). Therefore the earlier observation (that a sunny line cannot contain two points of \\(D_n\\)) is not violated.\n- The subsequent steps that attempt to derive a contradiction from the existence of the point \\(Q\\) are based on a misinterpretation of the observation.\n\nThus the reasoning that this configuration is impossible is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### 10. Alleged contradiction\n\n> “Adding the same vector once more yields  \n> \\[\n> Q=(2i-1,\\,2n+1-2i).\n> \\]  \n> … For any \\(i\\ge2\\) the point \\(Q\\) lies **outside** \\(T_n\\). However, the line \\(L\\) already contains the two distinct points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((i,n+1-i)\\) of \\(T_n\\); this contradicts the observation … that a sunny line cannot contain two points of the diagonal \\(D_n\\).”\n\n**Issue – Critical Error.** The contradiction is incorrectly stated:\n- The two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((i,n+1-i)\\) are **not both** in the diagonal \\(D_n\\); only the latter belongs to \\(D_n\\).\n- Consequently, there is no violation of the earlier “no two points of \\(D_n\\) on a sunny line” condition, and the argument fails to prove impossibility of \\(k=n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 11. Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) for \\(n=3\\)\n\n> “To see that \\(k=2\\) is impossible, note that any non‑sunny line is either horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\). A direct case analysis (enumerating the three possible choices for the single non‑sunny line) shows that the three remaining points cannot be covered by two sunny lines without violating the definition of sunny (the required slopes would be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).”\n\n**Issue – Justification Gap.** The solution asserts that a “direct case analysis” yields the impossibility, but it does **not** present the analysis. A rigorous proof would need to examine each of the three possibilities for the non‑sunny line and demonstrate that the remaining three points cannot be covered by two sunny lines. Since this is omitted, the claim is not fully justified.\n\n---\n\n### 12. Impossibility of \\(k=3\\) for \\(n=3\\)\n\n> “Similarly, with three sunny lines each line would have to contain at least two points of \\(T_3\\); a short inspection shows that any two points of \\(T_3\\) either share a prohibited direction or force a third point onto the same line, which again yields a prohibited slope.”\n\n**Issue – Justification Gap.** The argument is informal (“a short inspection”) and does not rigorously exclude all configurations of three sunny lines covering the six points. A complete proof would need to consider all possible assignments of points to lines and verify that a prohibited direction inevitably appears. The lack of such detail leaves a gap.\n\n---\n\n### 13. Overall conclusion (Section 6)\n\n> “These results give the exact answer for the smallest case and, for all larger \\(n\\), determine a large interval of feasible \\(k\\) together with a proof that the extreme value \\(k=n\\) cannot occur. The complete determination of the remaining borderline values (whether \\(k=n-1\\) is always attainable when \\(n\\ge4\\)) is left as an open sub‑problem.”\n\n**Issue – Critical Error.** Because the construction claimed for \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) (Section 3) is invalid, the statement that “all integers \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) are possible” is false. Moreover, the impossibility proof for \\(k=n\\) (Section 4) is also invalid. Hence the claimed “exact answer” for all \\(n\\ge4\\) is incorrect, and the problem remains unsolved. This renders the final classification erroneous.\n\n---\n\n### 14. Correct parts recap\n\n- The constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are valid.\n- The definitions of sunny lines and the basic properties of the set \\(T_n\\) are correct.\n- The observation that a sunny line cannot contain two points of the diagonal \\(D_n\\) is correct, but its misuse later leads to a false impossibility proof.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:** The solution correctly handles the cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\), but it contains **critical errors** in the central construction for \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) and in the impossibility proof for \\(k=n\\). Additionally, the impossibility arguments for the special case \\(n=3\\) are not fully justified (justification gaps). Consequently, the solutio