            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###  \n\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor an integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{b=1}^{n}(n+1-b)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the direction of the line \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A size bound for a sunny line inside a triangle  \n\nLet \\(m\\ge 1\\) and let \\(S\\subseteq T_m\\) be a set of points that lie on a\nsingle sunny line.  \nWrite the points of \\(S\\) as \\((a_i,b_i)\\) with the \\(a_i\\)’s strictly\nincreasing:\n\n\\[\na_1<a_2<\\dots <a_s .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is not parallel to the prohibited directions, two distinct\npoints of \\(S\\) cannot share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the same \\(y\\)-coordinate,\nnor the same sum \\(a+b\\).  \nHence the sums \\(a_i+b_i\\) are all distinct.  \nIf \\(a_{i+1}>a_i\\) then, to keep the sums distinct, we must have  \n\n\\[\nb_{i+1}<b_i .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the sequence \\((b_i)\\) is strictly decreasing.\n\nFrom the defining inequality of \\(T_m\\) we have  \n\n\\[\na_i+b_i\\le m+1\\qquad\\text{for every }i .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAdding (3) and (4) gives  \n\n\\[\na_i\\ge i,\\qquad b_i\\le m+1-a_i\\le m+1-i .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nb_i\\le m+1-i\\quad\\text{and}\\quad b_i\\ge s-i+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe two inequalities together imply  \n\n\\[\ns-i+1\\le m+1-i\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; s\\le m+1-i .\n\\]\n\nSince this must hold for \\(i=1\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\ns\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus **any sunny line meets \\(T_m\\) in at most \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points**. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Even numbers of sunny lines are impossible  \n\nAssume that for some even integer \\(k\\ge 2\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny\nlines covering all points of \\(T_k\\).  \nBy (6) each of those lines contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=\n\\frac{k}{2}\\) points.  Hence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot\\frac{k}{2}= \\frac{k^{2}}2 .\n\\]\n\nBut by (2)  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 = \\frac{k^{2}}2+\\frac{k}{2}> \\frac{k^{2}}2 .\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(k\\) sunny lines cannot cover all points of \\(T_k\\); a contradiction.\nTherefore **no even \\(k>0\\) is admissible**.  \nIn particular, when \\(n\\) is even the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Covering a triangle with an odd number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(k=2t+1\\) be odd (\\(t\\ge 0\\)).  \nConsider the family of lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;y=x+c\\qquad\\text{for }c=-t,-t+1,\\dots ,t .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAll these lines have slope \\(1\\); consequently each of them is sunny and the\n\\(k\\) lines are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\nFor a fixed \\(c\\) the integer points of \\(L_c\\) that belong to \\(T_k\\) are the\nsolutions of  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\na\\ge 1,\\;a+c\\ge 1,\\\\[2pt]\na+(a+c)\\le k+1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThese inequalities are equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le t+1-|c| .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(L_c\\) contains exactly \\(t+1-|c|\\) points of \\(T_k\\).  \nSumming (8) over all \\(c\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=-t}^{t}\\bigl(t+1-|c|\\bigr)=\\frac{(2t+1)(2t+2)}2\n      =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 =|T_k| .\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(k\\) lines in (7) together cover **every** point of \\(T_k\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  From the triangle to the original problem  \n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be given and let \\(k\\) be an odd integer with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n* **Step 5.1 – non‑sunny lines.**  \n  Use the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  H_j:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n  \\]\n\n  These lines are not sunny.\n\n* **Step 5.2 – the remaining points.**  \n  A point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) is not covered by the horizontal lines exactly when\n  \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\).  Writing \\(b'=b-(n-k)\\) we obtain  \n\n  \\[\n  a+b' \\le k+1,\\qquad a\\ge 1,\\;b'\\ge 1 .\n  \\]\n\n  Hence the uncovered set is the translate  \n\n  \\[\n  \\{(a,\\;b'+n-k)\\mid (a,b')\\in T_k\\}.\n  \\tag{9}\n  \\]\n\n* **Step 5.3 – sunny lines for the translate.**  \n  Apply the construction of Section 4 to the triangle \\(T_k\\) and then shift every\n  line upward by \\(n-k\\).  Explicitly, for each \\(c\\in\\{-t,\\dots ,t\\}\\) (\\(k=2t+1\\))\n  define  \n\n  \\[\n  S_c:\\;y = x + c + (n-k).\n  \\]\n\n  The family \\(\\{S_c\\}_{c=-t}^{t}\\) consists of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines and,\n  by (9), covers precisely the points that were left uncovered by the horizontal\n  lines.\n\nConsequently we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are\nsunny, that cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  The same argument works for\n\\(k=0\\) (use only the \\(n\\) horizontal lines).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final classification  \n\n* **Existence.**  \n  – \\(k=0\\) is always possible (all horizontal lines).  \n  – If \\(k\\) is odd and \\(1\\le k\\le n\\), the construction of Sections 5.1–5.3\n    yields a suitable family of lines.\n\n* **Non‑existence.**  \n  – If \\(k\\) is even and \\(k>0\\), Section 3 shows that covering the required\n    points with only sunny lines is impossible; therefore no configuration with\n    exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines can exist.\n\nPutting the two parts together we obtain the complete answer:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
