U\\subseteq T_n\\).  By translating the coordinate system we may assume that\n   \\(U=T_k\\) (the triangle defined by the same inequality with \\(k\\) in place of\n   \\(n\\)).  Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines must cover the whole set\n   \\(T_k\\).\n\n   Using (1) with \\(m=k\\) we obtain the upper bound  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The right\u2013hand side equals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\dfrac{k^{2}}2, & k\\text{ even},\\\\[4pt]\n   \\dfrac{k(k+1)}2, & k\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (2) is therefore impossible when \\(k\\) is even, while it holds\n   with equality when \\(k\\) is odd.  Hence\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{If a solution exists with }k>0,\\text{ then }k\\text{ must be odd}.}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The case \\(k=0\\) is trivially realizable (take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines\n   \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\)).\n\n4. **Partial constructions for odd\u202f\\(k\\).**  \n   *Even\u202f\\(n\\).*  \n   Let \\(k\\) be odd with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\); they are non\u2011sunny.\n   The points that remain uncovered are exactly a translate of \\(T_k\\):\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,\\;b+n-k)\\mid (a,b)\\in T_k\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   For the triangle \\(T_k\\) we can cover it with the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y = x + (i- \\tfrac{k+1}{2})\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,k),\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   each of slope\u202f\\(1\\) (hence sunny).  A direct verification shows that for\n   every \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) there is a unique index \\(i\\) with\n   \\(b = a + i-\\frac{k+1}{2}\\); consequently the family \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) covers\n   all points of \\(T_k\\) and the lines are distinct.  Shifting the whole\n   family upward by \\(n-k\\) gives \\(k\\) sunny lines covering the set (4).  Together\n   with the horizontal lines we obtain a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines\n   with exa