\(\\{a_i\\},\\{b_i\\},\\{a_i+b_i\\}\\) are all\n   disjoint, at most one of the three can contain more than\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) elements; otherwise two of\n   them would force the line to be parallel to one of the forbidden\n   directions.  This yields the sharp bound  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{any sunny line meets }T_m\\text{ in at most }\n          \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\text{ points}.}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Parity obstruction.**  \n   Assume a configuration with exactly \\(k>0\\) sunny lines exists.\n   Remove all non‑sunny lines; the uncovered points form a set\n   \\(U\\subseteq T_n\\).  By translating the coordinate system we may\n   assume that \\(U=T_k\\) (the triangle defined by the same inequality\n   with \\(k\\) in place of \\(n\\)).  Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines must\n   cover the whole set \\(T_k\\).\n\n   The points of the “hypotenuse’’ of \\(T_k\\),\n\n   \\[\n   H_k=\\{(i,k+1-i)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\},\n   \\]\n\n   are \\(k\\) distinct lattice points, each with sum of coordinates\n   equal to \\(k+1\\).  If a sunny line contained two points of \\(H_k\\),\n   the slope of the line would be \\(-1\\), which is forbidden.\n   Therefore **each sunny line can contain at most one point of\n   \\(H_k\\)**.  Because we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, each of them\n   must contain precisely one point of \\(H_k\\).\n\n   Consider the remaining points\n   \\(T_k\\setminus H_k\\).  Their number is \\(\\frac{k(k-1)}2\\).  By (1)\n   each sunny line can contain at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_k\\), and one\n   of those points is already a hypotenuse point.  Hence each sunny\n   line can cover at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor-1\\) interior points.\n   Consequently the total number of interior points that can be covered\n   by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   A short case analysis shows that (2) is **strictly smaller** than\n   \\(\\frac{k(k-1)}2\\) whenever \\(k\\) is even, while for odd \\(k\\) the two\n   quantities are equal.  Therefore the interior points cannot be\n   covered when \\(k\\) is even, and we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{if a solution exists with }k>0,\\text{ then }k\\text{ is odd}.}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The case \\(k=0\\) is trivially realizable (take the \\(n\\) horizontal\n   lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\)).\n\n4. **Why the construction in the previous answer fails.**  \n   The previous answer claimed that the \\(k\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y = x +\\bigl(i-\\tfrac{k+1}{2}\\bigr)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k)\n   \\]\n\n   cover all points of \\(T_k\\).  For even \\(k\\) the set of admissible\n   differences \\(b-a\\) contains the value \\(0\\), but none of the lines\n   \\(L_i\\) has intercept that yields \\(b-a=0\\); consequently points\n   such as \\((1,1)\\) (when \\(k\\ge2\\)) are left uncovered.  This shows\n   that the claimed construction does **not** work for even \\(k\\), in\n   agreement with the parity obstruction proved in (3).\n\n5. **Open part.**  \n   The parity obstruction (3) shows that the only possible values of\n   \\(k\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\text{ odd},\\qquad 0\\le k\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n   For odd \\(k\\) a concrete covering of \\(T_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines is\n   still missing; the simple family of slope‑\\(1\\) lines does not suffice,\n   and a more delicate construction is required.  Providing such a\n   construction (or proving that some odd \\(k\\) are impossible) remains\n   an open problem.  \n\n   Hence the complete description of all admissible \\(k\\) is not yet\n   established; only the necessary condition (3) and the trivial\n   construction for \\(k=0\\) have been proved rigorously. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now