he leftmost column of the triangle contains the \\(k\\) points  \n\n   \\[\n   C=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Two different points of \\(C\\) are vertically aligned; therefore a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(C\\).  \n   Because we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, each line must contain **exactly one** point of \\(C\\).\n\n   *Diagonal argument.*  \n   Points that lie on the same diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) are collinear on the line \\(x+y=s\\), whose slope is \\(-1\\) and is therefore forbidden.  \n   Hence a sunny line can contain at most one point from each diagonal.  \n   The set \\(T_k\\) has \\(k\\) different diagonals (the sums \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\)).  \n   Consequently each sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(C\\) **and** at most one point from each of the remaining \\(k-1\\) diagonals.\n\n   *Counting.*  \n   Let the \\(i\\)-th sunny line contain the column point \\((1,b_i)\\) and, besides that, at most one further point from each of the \\(k-1\\) other diagonals.  \n   Hence the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{column points})\\;+\\;k(k-1)\\;(\\text{other diagonal points})\\;=\\;k^2 .\n   \\]\n\n   The triangle \\(T_k\\) actually contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(k\\ge 4\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}2 > k^2\\qquad\\text{(equivalently }k+1>2k\\text{)},\n   \\]\n\n   which is impossible.  Hence no covering of \\(T_k\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists when \\(k\\ge 4\\).\n\n5. **Putting everything together.**  \n\n   * If \\(k=0\\) we use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).  \n   * If \\(k=1\\) we use \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines together with the sunny line \\(y=x\\).  \n   * If \\(k=3\\) we use \\(n-3\\) horizontal lines together with the three sunny lines displayed in step\u202f3.  \n   * The case \\(k=2\\) is impossible by step\u202f2, and every \\(k\\ge4\\) is impossibl