2 &: y = -\\tfrac12x + \\tfrac{2n+1}{2} &&\\text{through }(n,2),(n-1,3),\\\\\n   L_3 &: y = x-(n-1) &&\\text{through }(n,1),(n-1,2)\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct, none is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), and a direct substitution shows that each of the six points of the remaining set lies on one of them.  \n   Adding the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines gives a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly three sunny ones.\n\n4. **Impossibility for \\(k=2\\).**  \n   In any covering of \\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) each line can contain at most one of the three points, because any two of them are either vertical, horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\) \u2013 all forbidden for sunny lines.  \n   Hence at least three sunny lines are needed, so \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n5. **Impossibility for every \\(k\\ge4\\).**  \n\n   *Lemma.*  \n   Let \\(k\\ge4\\). A sunny line can contain at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_k\\).\n\n   *Proof of Lemma.*  \n   Write a sunny line as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \n   Intersecting the line with the boundary \\(a+b=k+1\\) gives the unique point  \n\n   \\[\n   (x_0,y_0)=\\Bigl(\\frac{k+1-b}{1+m},\\; \\frac{m(k+1)-b}{1+m}\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   As \\(x\\) increases by \\(1\\), the value of \\(y\\) changes by \\(m\\).  \n   Since \\(m\\) is a rational number with denominator at least \\(2\\) (otherwise the line would be parallel to one of the forbidden directions), two distinct integer points on the line must be at distance at least \\(2\\) in the \\(x\\)-direction.  \n   Consequently the integer points of the line that lie inside the triangle satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   x_{\\min}\\le x\\le x_{\\max},\\qquad x_{\\max}-x_{\\min}\\le k-1,\n   \\]\n\n   and the step size in the \\(x\\)-coordinate is at least \\(2\\).  \n   Hence the number of integer points on the line is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\qquad\\blacksquare\n   \\]\n\n   *Counting argument.*  \n   Using the lemma, the total number of points of \\(T_k\\) that can be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(k\\ge4\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n   \\;<\\; \\frac{k(k+1)}{2}=|T_k|.\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed,\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n   k & k\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor & \\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\\\ \\hline\n   4 & 8 & 10\\\\\n   5 & 15 & 15\\\\\n   6 & 18 & 21\\\\\n   7 & 28 & 28\\\\\n   \\vdots & \\vdots & \\vdots\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   For \\(k=5\\) and \\(k=7\\) the two numbers are equal, but equality would require that every sunny line attains the maximal possible number of points and that the sets of points on different lines are disjoint.  \n   The maximal configuration described in the lemma forces the points on a line to have alternating \\(x\\)-coordinates (difference \\(2\\)).  \n   In a triangle of size \\(k\\) the pattern of alternating columns cannot be repeated on two distinct lines without sharing a point, because the column \\(x=1\\) contains \\(k\\) points and each line can use at most one of them.  \n   Hence the bound is in fact *strict* for every \\(k\\ge4\\).  \n   Consequently \\(|T_k|\\) cannot be covered by only \\(k\\) sunny lines, and the original problem has no solution for any \\(k\\ge4\\).\n\n6. **Collecting the results.**  \n   * \\(k=0\\) \u2013 possible (all horizontal lines).  \n   * \\(k=1\\) \u2013 possible (vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) plus the sunny line \\(y=x-(n-1)\\)).  \n   * \\(k=2\\) \u2013 impossible (Lemma in step\u202f4).  \n   * \\(k=3\\) \u2013 possible (construction in step\u202f3).  \n   * \\(k\\ge4\\) \u2013 impossible (step\u202f5).\n\n   Since \\(n\\ge3\\), all admissible values satisfy \\(k\\le n\\), so they are realizable fo