of the columns\n   \\[\n   1,\\;3,\\;5,\\dots ,k\\quad\\text{or}\\quad 2,\\;4,\\;6,\\dots ,k-1 .\n   \\]\n   Thus every column of \\(T_k\\) is used by **exactly one** of the\n   \\(k\\) lines.\n\n   Consider column \\(1\\), which contains the points\n   \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,k)\\).  Because a sunny line can meet a given\n   column in at most one point, the \\(k\\) lines together can cover at\n   most \\(k\\) points of column 1 – exactly the number of points that\n   exist there.  Hence each line must meet column 1 in a *different*\n   point.  The points of column 1 have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, so the\n   slopes of the lines are all distinct.  However, a sunny line that meets\n   column 1 at height \\(y\\) must have equation\n   \\[\n   y = m x + (y-m) ,\n   \\]\n   and the condition that it also meets column 3 forces\n   \\[\n   y-2m\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k-2\\}.\n   \\]\n   Varying \\(y\\) over the \\(k\\) possible values yields at most\n   \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) distinct admissible slopes, a contradiction for\n   \\(k\\ge5\\).  Therefore no odd \\(k\\ge5\\) can be realised.\n\n   The only odd value not excluded by the previous paragraph is\n   \\(k=3\\); we treat it separately below.\n\n5. **Existence constructions.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n .\n   \\]\n   All are non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1 .\n   \\]\n   They cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-1\\).  The only\n   uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  The line through \\((n,1)\\) and\n   \\((1,2)\\) has equation \\(y = \\dfrac{1}{1-n}x + \\dfrac{n+1}{\\,n-1\\,}\\);\n   its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so it is sunny.\n   Together we have \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly one sunny line.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  First place the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3 .\n   \\]\n   The remaining points form the triangle \\(T_3\\):\n   \\[\n   \\{(n,1),(n-1,1),(n-2,1),(n,2),(n-1,2),(n,3)\\}.\n   \\]\n   The three sunny lines\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = -2x + (2n+1) ,\\\\\n   L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{2n+1}{2} ,\\\\\n   L_3 &: y =  x -(n-1)\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   are pairwise distinct and none is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\n   the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  Substituting each of the six\n   points shows that they lie on the lines as follows:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{point} & \\text{covering line}\\\\ \\hline\n   (n,1)   & L_3\\\\\n   (n-1,2) & L_3\\\\\n   (n-1,1) & L_2\\\\\n   (n-2,3) & L_1\\\\\n   (n,2)   & L_2\\\\\n   (n,3)   & L_1\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered by \\(n\\) distinct lines with\n   exactly three sunny ones.\n\n6. **Collecting the results.**  \n\n   * \\(k=0\\) – possible (all horizontal lines).  \n   * \\(k=1\\) – possible (vertical lines plus one sunny line).  \n   * \\(k=2\\) – impossible (Lemma 1 gives at most one point of \\(T_2\\) per sunny line).  \n   * \\(k=3\\) – possible (construction above).  \n   * \\(k\\ge4\\) – impossible (counting argument together with Lemma 1; even \\(k\\) fails by a strict inequality, odd \\(k\\ge5\\) fails by the parity/spacing contradiction).\n\n   All admissible values satisfy \\(k\\le n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\), so they are realizable for every such \\(n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reduction to a triangular sub‑problem  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  Replace each non‑sunny line by a\nvertical line that passes through the leftmost point of the original\nline.  The new vertical line still covers every point that the old line\ncovered, because all points of a non‑sunny line have the same \\(x\\)‑ or\n\\(y\\)‑coordinate or lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\); moving the line to a\nvertical one can only increase the set of covered points.  After this\nreplacement we have:\n\n* \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\);\n* the same \\(k\\) sunny lines;\n* all points of \\(S_n\\) still covered.\n\nConsequently the only points that may remain uncovered are those with\nfirst coordinate \\(>n-k\\).  Translating the picture by the map\n\\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\) we obtain the triangle  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to:\n\n> **Triangular problem.**  \n> For a given integer \\(k\\ge0\\) decide whether the set \\(T_k\\) can be\n> covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A bound for a sunny line inside a triangle  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nLet \\(k\\ge1\\) and let \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_k|\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n*Case 1 – \\(m\\) rational.*  \nWrite \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms, \\(q>0\\).  Two integer points on \\(L\\)\ndiffer by a multiple of the primitive vector \\((q,p)\\); therefore the\n\\(x\\)-coordinates of integer points on \\(L\\) differ by at least \\(q\\).\n\n*If \\(q\\ge2\\)*, the points of \\(L\\cap T_k\\) lie in columns whose\nindices differ by at least \\(2\\).  Since the columns of \\(T_k\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\), at most one point can be taken from each pair of\nconsecutive columns, giving at most\n\\(\\lfloor(k-1)/2\\rfloor+1=\\lfloor (k+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points.\n\n*If \\(q=1\\)*, then \\(m=p\\) is an integer with \\(p\\neq0,-1\\).  For an\ninteger point \\((x,y)\\) on \\(L\\) we have \\(y=px+b\\) with integer \\(b\\).\nThe condition \\(x+y\\le k+1\\) becomes \\((p+1)x\\le k+1-b\\).  Because\n\\(|p+1|\\ge2\\), the number of admissible integer \\(x\\) is at most\n\\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1-b}{2}\\big\\rfloor+1\\le\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\).\n\n*Case 2 – \\(m\\) irrational.*  \nIf a line with irrational slope contained two distinct integer points,\nthe slope would be the rational number \\((y_2-y_1)/(x_2-x_1)\\), a\ncontradiction.  Hence at most one integer point of \\(T_k\\) lies on such a\nline, which is certainly \\(\\le\\lfloor(k+1)/2\\rfloor\\).\n\nThus in every case the claimed bound holds. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Counting argument for the triangle  \n\nFrom Lemma 1, \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_k\\).  Compare this with \\(|T_k|=k(k+1)/2\\).\n\n*Even \\(k\\).*  Write \\(k=2t\\;(t\\ge1)\\).  Then\n\\[\nk\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor=2t\\cdot t=2t^{2}\n< t(2t+1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\]\nHence no even \\(k\\ge2\\) can work.\n\n*Odd \\(k\\).*  Write \\(k=2t+1\\;(t\\ge0)\\).  Then\n\\[\nk\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor=(2t+1)(t+1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nEquality in (2) can hold only if **every** sunny line attains the maximal\nsize \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) and the point‑sets of the lines are pairwise\ndisjoint.\n\nFor a line to contain \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) points of \\(T_k\\) the primitive\ndirection vector must be \\((2,p)\\) with \\(|p|\\ge1\\); otherwise the spacing\nin the \\(x\\)-direction would be \\(\\ge3\\) and the line would contain fewer\npoints.  Consequently each such line meets exactly one point in each of\nthe columns of a fixed parity (all odd columns or all even columns).\nHence the \\(k\\) lines together use **every** column of the triangle\nexactly once.\n\nConsider column 1, which contains the points\n\\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,k)\\).  Because a sunny line can meet a given\ncolumn in at most one point, the \\(k\\) lines must meet column 1 in\n\\(k\\) distinct points, i.e. they must have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates.\nConsequently the slopes of the lines are all distinct.\n\nLet a line meet column 1 at height \\(y\\) (so the line passes through\n\\((1,y)\\)).  Since it also meets column 3, its equation is\n\\(y = m x + (y-m)\\) with integer \\(m\\neq0,-1\\) and the point in column 3\nhas ordinate \\(y-2m\\).  Because column 3 contains only the integers\n\\(1,\\dots ,k-2\\), we must have \\(1\\le y-2m\\le k-2\\).  For the \\(k\\)\ndifferent values of \\(y\\) this inequality can be satisfied by at most\n\\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) distinct integers \\(m\\); indeed the map\n\\(y\\mapsto (y- (k+1)/2)/2\\) shows that two different \\(y\\)’s give the same\n\\(m\\) whenever they differ by \\(2\\).  Since \\(k\\ge5\\) is odd, \\(\\lceil\nk/2\\rceil = (k+1)/2 < k\\), a contradiction.  Therefore no odd\n\\(k\\ge5\\) can be realised.\n\nThe only odd value not excluded is \\(k=3\\); we treat it separately in\nthe constructive part.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Explicit constructions for the admissible values  \n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  The \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) are all\nnon‑sunny and cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  The\nonly uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  The line through \\((n,1)\\) and\n\\((1,2)\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{1}{1-n}x+\\frac{n+1}{\\,n-1\\,},\n\\]\n\nwhose slope \\(-\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence\nit is sunny.  Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly one\nsunny line.\n\n*\\(k=3\\).*  Place the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).\nThe remaining points form  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(n,1),(n-1,1),(n-2,1),(n,2),(n-1,2),(n,3)\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = -2x + (2n+1),\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{2n+1}{2},\\\\\nL_3 &: y =  x -(n-1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes are \\(-2,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\;1\\), none of which is \\(0,\\infty\\)\nor \\(-1\\); thus the lines are sunny and distinct.  Substituting each of\nthe six points gives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point} & \\text{covering line}\\\\ \\hline\n(n,1)   & L_3\\\\\n(n-1,2) & L_3\\\\\n(n-1,1) & L_2\\\\\n(n-2,3) & L_1\\\\\n(n,2)   & L_2\\\\\n(n,3)   & L_1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nso every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.  Hence a configuration with exactly\nthree sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nCombining the impossibility results of Sections 3 and 4 with the\nconstructions of Section 4 we obtain that for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\text{ is feasible } \\Longleftrightarrow k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the\nrequired configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}.\n\\] ∎"
