013 If \\(q\\ge2\\) then at most one point of \\(L\\) can lie in each pair of\n   consecutive columns, so at most \\(\\lfloor(k-1)/2\\rfloor+1=\n     \\lfloor (k+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points belong to \\(T_k\\).  \n   \u2013 If \\(q=1\\) then \\(m\\) is an integer \\(p\\neq0,-1\\).  \n     For an integer point \\((x,y)\\) on \\(L\\) we have \\(y=px+b\\) with\n     integer \\(b\\).  The condition \\(x+y\\le k+1\\) becomes\n     \\((p+1)x\\le k+1-b\\).  Since \\(|p+1|\\ge2\\), the number of admissible\n     integer \\(x\\) is at most \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1-b}{2}\\big\\rfloor+1\\le\n     \\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\).  \n   The case of an irrational slope is handled by approximating the\n   line with rational slopes; any two integer points would then give a\n   rational slope, contradicting irrationality, so at most one integer\n   point can occur \u2013 which is certainly \\(\\le\\lfloor(k+1)/2\\rfloor\\). \u220e\n\n4. **Counting argument.**  \n   From Lemma\u202f1, \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_k\\).  Compare this with \\(|T_k|=k(k+1)/2\\).\n\n   *If \\(k\\) is even*, write \\(k=2t\\).  Then\n   \\[\n   k\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor=2t\\cdot t=2t^{2}\n   <t(2t+1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   Hence an even \\(k\\ge2\\) is impossible.\n\n   *If \\(k\\) is odd*, write \\(k=2t+1\\).  Then\n   \\[\n   k\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor=(2t+1)(t+1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   Equality can hold only if **every** sunny line attains the maximal\n   number \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) of points and the point\u2011sets of the lines are\n   pairwise disjoint.\n\n   For a line to contain \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) points of \\(T_k\\) it must have\n   primitive direction vector \\((2,\\,p)\\) with \\(|p|\\ge1\\) (otherwise the\n   spacing in the \\(x\\)-direction would be \\(>2\\) and the line would\n   contain fewer points).  Consequently each such line meets exactly