, so the \\(k\\) lines meet\n   column 1 in the distinct points \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,k)\\).  Hence the\n   slopes of the lines are the numbers\n\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{y_i-y_j}{c_i-1}\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k),\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(y_i\\) is the ordinate of the point of line \\(i\\) in column 1 and\n   \\(c_i\\) is the smallest column (different from 1) used by that line.\n   Because each line uses only columns of a single parity, the integer\n   \\(c_i-1\\) is **odd**.  Therefore each slope \\(m_i\\) is a rational number\n   whose denominator (in lowest terms) is odd.\n\n   Now look at column 2.  It must be used by exactly \\(k-1\\) lines, i.e. by all\n   lines **except one**.  The line that skips column 2 therefore uses only odd\n   columns (1,3,5,…).  Its slope has denominator \\(2\\) (the difference between\n   columns 1 and 3), which is even – a contradiction, because we have just\n   proved that every slope must have an odd denominator.  Hence no odd\n   \\(k\\ge5\\) can satisfy the required conditions.\n\n6. **The only possible odd value, \\(k=3\\).**  \n   For \\(k=3\\) the parity argument above does not lead to a contradiction,\n   and an explicit construction exists.\n\n   *Construction for \\(k=3\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3 .\n   \\]\n\n   The points that are still uncovered are exactly the six points of the\n   triangle \\(T_3\\):\n\n   \\[\n   (n,1),\\;(n-1,1),\\;(n-2,1),\\;(n,2),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,3).\n   \\]\n\n   The three sunny lines\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = -2x + (2n+1),\\\\\n   L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{2n+1}{2},\\\\\n   L_3 &: y =  x -(n-1)\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct and none is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\n   the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  Substituting each of the six points\n   gives the following incidences:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{point} & \\text{covering line}\\\\ \\hline\n   (n,1)   & L_3\\\\\n   (n-1,2) & L_3\\\\\n   (n-1,1) & L_2\\\\\n   (n-2,1) & L_1\\\\\n   (n,2)   & L_2\\\\\n   (n,3)   & L_1\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered by \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly\n   three sunny ones.\n\n7. **Existence for the even‑\\(k\\) cases \\(k=0,1\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\); all are\n   non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  The only\n   uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  The line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\)\n   has equation \\(y=\\dfrac{1}{1-n}x+\\dfrac{n+1}{n-1}\\); its slope\n   \\(-\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so it is sunny.\n   Thus we have exactly one sunny line.\n\n8. **Collecting the results.**  \n\n   * impossible:* every even \\(k\\ge2\\) and every odd \\(k\\ge5\\);  \n   * possible:* \\(k=0,1,3\\) (the constructions above work for every\n   \\(n\\ge3\\)).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}}\n\\] \n\n∎"
