n\n   If \\(m\\) is rational, write \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms (\\(q>0\\)).  \n   Integer points on \\(L\\) differ by the primitive vector \\((q,p)\\); hence the\n   \\(x\\)-coordinates of points of \\(L\\cap T_k\\) differ by at least \\(q\\).\n\n   *If \\(q\\ge2\\)*, at most one point of \\(L\\) can lie in each pair of\n   consecutive columns, so at most \\(\\lfloor(k-1)/2\\rfloor+1=\n   \\lfloor (k+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points belong to \\(T_k\\).\n\n   *If \\(q=1\\)*, then \\(m=p\\) is an integer with \\(p\\neq0,-1\\).  \n   For a point \\((x,y)\\) on \\(L\\) we have \\(y=px+b\\) with integer \\(b\\).  \n   The condition \\(x+y\\le k+1\\) becomes \\((p+1)x\\le k+1-b\\).  \n   Because \\(|p+1|\\ge2\\), the number of admissible integer \\(x\\) is at most\n   \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1-b}{2}\\big\\rfloor+1\\le\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\).\n\n   Thus in every case \\(|L\\cap T_k|\\le\\lfloor (k+1)/2\\rfloor\\). ∎\n\n\n\n3. **Counting argument.**  \n   From Lemma 1, \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_k\\).  Since \\(|T_k|=k(k+1)/2\\),\n\n   *if \\(k\\) is even*, write \\(k=2t\\;(t\\ge1)\\).  Then the left‑hand side of (1)\n   equals \\(2t\\cdot t=2t^{2}\\) while the right‑hand side equals\n   \\(\\frac{2t(2t+1)}2=t(2t+1)=2t^{2}+t\\).  Hence (1) is **strictly smaller**,\n   so no even \\(k\\ge2\\) can work.\n\n   *if \\(k\\) is odd*, write \\(k=2t+1\\).  Then (1) equals\n   \\((2t+1)(t+1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\); consequently every sunny line must attain\n   the maximal size \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) and the point‑sets of the \\(k\\) lines must\n   be pairwise disjoint.\n\n4. **Structure of a maximal line.**  \n   Let \\(L\\) be a sunny line that meets \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) points of \\(T_k\\).\n   By Lemma 1 the primitive direction vector of \\(L\\) must be \\((2,p)\\) with\n   \\(p\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0,-2\\}\\); otherwise the horizontal step would be\n   \\(